Here is a piece of code from a computer program. Examine it closely and make a list of any potential weaknesses, organizing them based on the OWASP WSTG guidelines.

### services/token.js

This function retrieves a refresh token that is saved on your computer.
```
const getLocalRefreshToken = () => {
 return localStorage.getItem("refresh\_token");
};
```

This function saves a refresh token on your computer.
```
const setLocalRefreshToken = (token) => {
 localStorage.setItem("refresh\_token", token);
};
```

This function retrieves an access token that is saved on your computer.
```
const getLocalAccessToken = () => {
 return localStorage.getItem("access\_token");
};
```

This function saves an access token on your computer.
```
const updateLocalAccessToken = (token) => {
 localStorage.setItem("access\_token", token);
};
```

This function retrieves user information that is saved on your computer. It changes the text into a usable format.
```
const getUser = () => {
 return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("user"));
};
```

This function saves user information on your computer. It changes the information into a text format before saving.
```
const setUser = (user) => {
 console.log(JSON.stringify(user));
 localStorage.setItem("user", JSON.stringify(user));
};
```

This function removes user information, the access token, and the refresh token from your computer's storage.
```
const removeUser = () => {
 localStorage.removeItem("user");
 localStorage.removeItem("access\_token");
 localStorage.removeItem("refresh\_token");
};
```

Here's a collection of the functions described above:
```
const TokenService = {
 getLocalRefreshToken,
 setLocalRefreshToken,
 getLocalAccessToken,
 updateLocalAccessToken,
 getUser,
 setUser,
 removeUser,
};
```

Finally, this line makes these functions available for use in other parts of the program.
```
export default TokenService;
```

